skeptic:
The error is real. L103 says the lane 'is chosen by PA[7:6]' for every request. The RTL it cites (core-et-main shirecache_mesh_master.sv:148 and :152) sets the lane from req_bank_id: PA[7:6] only for a remote-scratchpad request, otherwise the L3 bank from the swizzle. The default swizzle (core-et esr_cache_bank.v:88-90, mode 0) puts the bank just above the 5 shire bits, i.e. PA[12:11]. That matches how the spec routes L3 requests (pdf p.6). ml:l3:l3.lane on the same page says so, and the memory levels' conflicts list says L103 'needs the correction'. The Erbium drop has no shire-cache pipeline RTL, so the re-implementation is the only RTL basis, and silicon has not confirmed it.

The fix is incomplete. The same rule is drawn or animated in four more places on the chip page:
- the shire drawing's label 'lane = PA[7:6]' (chip-diagram.script.js:373);
- the meshstop panel, 'a line takes lane PA[7:6]' (:1096);
- the Load→DRAM flow, which sends the example line out on lane P1.lane[c.a.bank] (:1690). For 0x80_1234_5340, PA[7:6] = 1 but PA[12:11] = 2;
- the mesh scale's to_l3 master, drawn 'lane = PA[7:6]' (circuitkit.tail.js:45 → circuitkit.js:1642). This contradicts the l3.lane text in its own panel: shot scale-086-mesh.png against the crawled panel mesh/tol3.

Also, 'add caveat reimpl' cannot be done for a chip fact. norm() in the chip's build_facts.py:615 does not carry 'caveat', and none of the 304 chip facts has one, so the caveat has to go in the statement.

s02's mesh-lane-is-not-pa76-for-l3 (rated major) covers :373, :1096, :1690 and L103 but not the mesh scale; merge them.

